  "worked_solution": "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+4y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z & \\,\u27f9\\,{u}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=1 & \\,\u27f9\\,v=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}\u2061z\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-\\int \\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-z & =x+C \\\\ z(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061z-1) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4y)(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+4y)-1)=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=(x+4y)(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+4y)-1).$",
